edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152314 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186049935 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152314 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "26b78e45-1997-563c-b456-d53ae655f749" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152870 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186151727 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152870 #edb/ref 1069 "q-93603" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152870 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152870 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following numbers are integers?\n\nI. $1$\n\nII. $-2$\n\nIII. $-\\frac{1}{2}$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152870 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "39369502-10cb-5b34-b9b4-f444ee2b4bd4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152870 #edb/ref 1074 "The integers consist of all positive and negative whole numbers and zero.\n\n$$\n…,-3,\\,-2,\\,-1,\\,0,\\,1,\\,2,\\,3,…\n$$\n\n \n\nOf the given numbers, $1$ and $-2$ are integers, but $-\\frac{1}{2}$ is not.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153002 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186153003 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153002 #edb/ref 1061 172680 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153002 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "0fa6983b-4c2c-5789-8e95-585a4e680b9e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153002 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186153465 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153003 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186153004 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153003 #edb/ref 1061 172681 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153003 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "bc685578-51b3-5ffb-b8b8-a5cca744297f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153003 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186152124 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153004 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186153005 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153004 #edb/ref 1061 172684 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153004 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "bfd8720b-23e8-55fe-8c02-ac787976007a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153004 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186153466 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153005 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186153006 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153005 #edb/ref 1061 172685 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153005 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "34d04639-2557-5679-9ab1-fa22b4733ce3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153005 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186152125 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153006 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186153007 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153006 #edb/ref 1061 172688 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153006 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "cc715ab1-8b8b-555b-bd73-97d8d33107c0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153006 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186153467 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153007 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186153008 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153007 #edb/ref 1061 172689 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153007 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "ba79c281-b939-528f-8605-0a6cd2aa6abd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153007 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186152126 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153008 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186153009 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153008 #edb/ref 1061 172692 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153008 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "8cd64bc9-e39e-5f5c-9c0d-618b3294cff3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153008 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186153468 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153009 #edb/ref 1061 172693 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153009 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "7c946365-2b4a-5987-84b7-84cce8c8a6f4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153009 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186152127 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153465 #edb/ref 1064 "The **natural numbers** are the positive whole numbers:\n${\\color{blue}1},\\: {\\color{blue}2},\\: {\\color{blue}3},\\: {\\color{blue}4},\\: {\\color{blue}5},\\ldots$\nWe also refer to them as the **counting numbers**.\n\nThere's some debate among mathematicians about whether zero should be considered a natural number. However, for our purposes, zero is *not* a natural number." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153465 #edb/ref 1065 9069 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153465 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "ddcec145-4774-553b-836d-76a4a5cd221e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153465 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153466 #edb/ref 1064 "When we extend the natural numbers by joining them with zero and the whole negative numbers, we get the **integers**.\n$\\overbrace{\\ldots, {\\color{red}-5},\\: {\\color{red}-4},\\: {\\color{red}-3},\\: {\\color{red}-2},\\: {\\color{red}-1},\\: {\\color{red}0}, \\: \\!\\underbrace{{\\color{blue}1},\\: {\\color{blue}2}, \\: {\\color{blue}3},\\: {\\color{blue}4},\\: {\\color{blue}5},\\ldots}_{\\large\\textrm{Natural numbers}}}^{\\large\\textrm{Integers}}$\n\nWe can represent the relationship between the natural numbers and integers using an inclusion diagram:\n\n![](images/00/00a2725bbe34eb8bc88f7f6dca6f22e624854a0cb469645eb2f4f9b79a38f846.png)\n\nNotice that the natural numbers are *contained within* the integers. In other words, *every* natural number is also an integer." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153466 #edb/ref 1065 9070 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153466 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "4109da7f-f9b5-5c94-8215-06f5ea408038" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153466 #edb/ref 1067 "The Integers" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153467 #edb/ref 1064 "The **rational numbers** consist of all numbers that can be written as the ratio of two integers. For example:\n\n$$\n{\\color{purple}-\\dfrac{1}{2}}, \\quad {\\color{purple}\\dfrac{1}{2}}, \\quad {\\color{purple}\\dfrac{1}{3}}, \\quad {\\color{purple}-\\dfrac{2}{3}}, \\quad {\\color{purple}\\dfrac{2}{3}}\n$$\n\nEvery integer is also a rational number because every integer can be expressed as a ratio of two integers. For example,\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}2} = {\\color{black}\\dfrac{2}{1}}, \\qquad {\\color{red}-9} = {\\color{black}\\dfrac{(-9)}{\\phantom{-}1}}\n$$\n\nLikewise, any mixed number is a rational number because it can be written as an improper fraction. For example,\n\n$$\n2 \\, \\dfrac{1}{3}\n$$\n\nis a rational number because\n\n$$\n2 \\, \\dfrac{1}{3} = \\dfrac{2 \\times 3 + 1}{3} = \\dfrac{7}{3}\n$$\n\nWe can now add the rational numbers to our inclusion diagram.\n\n![](images/12/124ced6b317bdd2df4f967e6ea45e613b89d9fa136acb5edf8f7ed86fa8a4c06.png)\n\nNotice that the integers (and therefore the natural numbers) are contained within the rational numbers." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153467 #edb/ref 1065 9071 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153467 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "3563ca37-8d82-5742-92b2-bb3918a71477" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153467 #edb/ref 1067 "The Rational Numbers" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153468 #edb/ref 1064 "Let's consider the following rational number:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac13\n$$\n\nWe can find the decimal representation of this rational number by dividing $1$ by $3$ using long division, as follows:\n\n| $\\,\\,0 \\,. 3 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 3 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 3$$\\:\\ldots$$3$$\\!\\require{enclose}\\enclose{longdiv}{1 {\\,. \\,} 0 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 0 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 0}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$9$$1$$0$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$9$$1$$0$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$9$$1$$\\,\\ldots$ |\n| --- |\n\nNote that, in this case\n\n- the long division process never ends, and\n- the digit $3$ after the decimal point repeats *forever*.\n\nTherefore, we have that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac13 = 0.333\\,333\\ldots\n$$\n\nThis is an example of a **repeating decimal**. A repeating decimal is a decimal where the digits after the decimal point continue forever but in a repeating pattern.\n\nIt's convenient to use the following special notation to indicate decimals that repeat indefinitely.\n\n$$\n0.\\overline{3} = 0.333\\,333\\ldots\n$$\n\nSome decimals have two or more digits that repeat indefinitely. For example:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n0.78^(―) &= 0.78787878… \\\\\n0.675^(―) &= 0.675675675…\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSome repeating decimals have non-repeating digits before the repeating part. For example:\n\n$$\n0.333\\overline{67} = 0.333\\,67\\,67\\,67\\,67\\ldots\n$$\n\nThe key takeaway for this section is that every repeating decimal is rational! In other words, every repeating decimal can be expressed as a ratio of two integers.\n\nFor example:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n0.3^(―) &= \\frac{1}{3} \\\\\n0.78^(―) &= \\frac{26}{33} \\\\\n0.33367^(―) &= \\frac{16517}{49500}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn a future lesson, we'll learn how to express a repeating decimal as a fraction. For now, you can verify these results using a calculator." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153468 #edb/ref 1065 9072 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153468 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "28ff4afd-e8c7-511c-86a4-14d547e6c6d2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153468 #edb/ref 1067 "Repeating Decimals" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168729 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186168730 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168729 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186168731 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168729 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186168732 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168729 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186168733 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168729 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186168734 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168729 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168729 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168729 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186168734 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168729 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "c4005020-e950-5306-a22c-5bc62b40c40b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168730 #edb/ref 1080 "II and III only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168730 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168730 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "ca7235df-ed54-56b4-a96a-f4dd62411d9e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168731 #edb/ref 1080 "III only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168731 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168731 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "09345208-4c96-5ff8-aad9-4dc79f7055c4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168732 #edb/ref 1080 "II only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168732 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168732 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "3f01a342-66a4-565d-8547-c05a295e8ace" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168733 #edb/ref 1080 "None" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168733 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168733 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "48110931-110b-5b28-8961-d5731a2dc0ad" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168734 #edb/ref 1080 "I, II, and III" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168734 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168734 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "4b893675-cde8-51e7-879a-c1367e13459f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186171932 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048145 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186171932 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048967 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186171932 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186172310 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186171932 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186172602 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186171932 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186432788 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186171932 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186432792 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186171932 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186432793 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186171932 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186432794 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186171932 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186515522 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186171932 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186523999 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186171932 #edb/ref 1039 "Determining if a Number Is Divisible by 3 or 9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186171932 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186171756 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186171932 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "fb8fa39c-a982-569a-b2e0-833c51259711" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186171933 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186172311 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186171933 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186172603 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186171933 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186432783 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186171933 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186432785 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186171933 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186432786 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186171933 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186432789 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186171933 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186432791 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186171933 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186483402 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186171933 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186483410 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186171933 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186518187 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186171933 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186527149 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186171933 #edb/ref 1039 "Determining if a Number Is Divisible by 6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186171933 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186171757 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186171933 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "b483f9f7-47a9-5725-b1f1-fe0ea39a7e96" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186171934 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186172454 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186171934 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186172604 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186171934 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186432784 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186171934 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186432787 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186171934 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186432790 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186171934 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186432795 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186171934 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186432796 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186171934 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186521495 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186171934 #edb/ref 1039 "Determining if a Number Is Divisible by 7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186171934 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186171758 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186171934 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "cb0ae539-5348-5a2c-b226-c009ce6d6094" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172213 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186172716 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172213 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186172717 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172213 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186172718 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172213 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186172719 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172213 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186172720 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172213 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186172721 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172213 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172213 #edb/ref 1045 "Divisibility of Integers by 3, 9, 6, and 7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172213 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186172716 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172213 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186050318 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172213 #edb/ref 1050 540.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172213 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "d1fb6020-a735-5706-80b8-bc04b0d8b879" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172716 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186172717 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172716 #edb/ref 1061 172668 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172716 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "1794b655-2bac-5203-8fdb-02da477fb69d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172716 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186173299 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172717 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186172718 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172717 #edb/ref 1061 172669 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172717 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "b30e2ba3-941c-5d16-b861-02db07423b35" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172717 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186171932 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172718 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186172719 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172718 #edb/ref 1061 172672 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172718 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "96c70f43-c735-5619-a8c7-49afb6e904cd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172718 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186173304 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172719 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186172720 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172719 #edb/ref 1061 172673 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172719 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "46310e8f-1f56-5e58-9836-71090df92551" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172719 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186171933 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172720 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186172721 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172720 #edb/ref 1061 172676 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172720 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "8c02867b-43a4-5116-88da-bc7736bfb7a5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172720 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186173300 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172721 #edb/ref 1061 172677 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172721 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "777acec7-1e70-587a-80c0-746fd1cdb9e4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172721 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186171934 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173299 #edb/ref 1064 "In a previous lesson, we studied some rules for determining if an integer is divisible by $2,5,10,4,$ and $8.$ In this lesson, we'll extend our understanding of divisibility rules to include $3,9,6,$ and $7.$\n\nWe have the following rule for determining if a number is divisible by $3{:}$\n\n*A number is divisible by $3$ if the sum of its digits is divisible by $3.$*\n\nFor example, let's determine if the following number is divisible by $3{:}$\n\n$$\n312\\,561\n$$\n\nIf we sum the digits, we get\n\n$$\n3+1+2+5+6+1 = 18.\n$$\n\nNow, since $18$ is divisible by $3,$ we conclude that $312\\,561$ is divisible by $3.$\n\nThe rule for determining if a number is divisible by $9$ is similar:\n\n*A number is divisible by $9$ if the sum of its digits is divisible by $9.$*\n\nLet's see another example." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173299 #edb/ref 1065 9390 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173299 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "0b0f6b89-66c8-5e63-9825-60eb62f1271f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173299 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173300 #edb/ref 1064 "The rule for dividing by $7$ is the trickiest rule of the ones we've studied.\n\n*To check if a number is divisible by $7,$ we double the last digit, subtract the result from the remaining digits, and then check if the difference is divisible by $7.$*\n\nFor example, let's check if the following number is divisible by $7{:}$\n\n$$\n46{\\color{red}{2}}\n$$\n\nThe last digit is ${\\color{red}{2}}.$ If we double this digit, we get $2\\cdot {\\color{red}{2}} =  {\\color{blue}{4}}.$ Then, if we subtract ${\\color{blue}{4}}$ from the remaining digits, we get\n\n$$\n46 - {\\color{blue}{4}} = 42.\n$$\n\nNow, since $42$ is divisible by $7,$ we conclude that $462$ is divisible by $7.$\n\nLet's see some more examples." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173300 #edb/ref 1065 9391 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173300 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "a5cdfa6e-09a2-5600-9083-a424aaf3d959" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173300 #edb/ref 1067 "Divisibility by 7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173304 #edb/ref 1064 "We have the following rule for determining if a number is divisible by $6{:}$\n\n*A number is divisible by $6$ if it is divisible by both $2$ and $3.$*\n\nFor example, let's determine if the following number is divisible by $6{:}$\n\n$$\n353,856\n$$\n\nTo check whether this number is divisible by $6,$ we need to check that it's divisible by *both* $2$ *and* $3{:}$\n\n- Notice that the last digit of $353,85{\\color{blue}{6}}$ is ${\\color{blue}{6}}.$ Since ${\\color{blue}{6}}$ is even, $353,856$ is divisible by $2.\\quad{\\color{green}{\\checkmark}}$\n\n- We now check for divisibility by $3.$ The sum of the digits is\n\n$$\n3+5+3+8+5+6 = 30.\n$$\n\nSince $30$ is divisible by $3,$ we have that $353,856$ is divisible by $3.\\quad{\\color{green}{\\checkmark}}$\n\nSo, since $353,856$ is divisible by *both* $2$ *and* $3,$ we conclude that it is divisible by $6.$\n\nLet's see another example." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173304 #edb/ref 1065 9441 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173304 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "955bbb9e-e7e0-552d-9ecf-ef5efd79239a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173304 #edb/ref 1067 "Divisibility by 6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186177303 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186177304 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186177303 #edb/ref 1075 #e